      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 1407 and 903, we can use the Euclidean algorithm, which involves a series of division steps with remainder. The Euclidean algorithm states that the GCD of two numbers also divides their difference.\n\nLet's apply the Euclidean algorithm to 1407 and 903:\n\n1. Divide 1407 by 903 and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   1407 = 903 \\cdot 1 + 504\n   \\]\n   Here, the remainder is 504.\n\n2. Now, divide 903 by 504:\n   \\[\n   903 = 504 \\cdot 1 + 399\n   \\]\n   Here, the remainder is 399.\n\n3. Next, divide 504 by 399:\n   \\[\n   504 = 399 \\cdot 1 + 105\n   \\]\n   Here, the remainder is 105.\n\n4. Then, divide 399 by 105:\n   \\[\n   399 = 105 \\cdot 3 + 94\n   \\]\n   Here, the remainder is 94.\n\n5. Now, divide 105 by 94:\n   \\[\n   105 = 94 \\cdot 1 + 11\n   \\]\n   Here, the remainder is 11.\n\n6. Next, divide 94 by 11:\n   \\[\n   94 = 11 \\cdot 8 + 6\n   \\]\n   Here, the remainder is 6.\n\n7. Then, divide 11 by 6:\n   \\[\n   11 = 6 \\cdot 1 + 5\n   \\]\n   Here, the remainder is 5.\n\n8. Now, divide 6 by 5:\n   \\[\n   6 = 5 \\cdot 1 + 1\n   \\]\n   Here, the remainder is 1.\n\n9. Finally, divide 5 by 1:\n   \\[\n   5 = 1 \\cdot 5 + 0\n   \\]\n   Here, the remainder is 0.\n\nWhen the remainder becomes 0, the divisor at that step is the GCD. Therefore, the GCD of 1407 and 903 is the last non-zero remainder, which is 1.\n\nThus, the greatest common divisor of 1407 and 903 is \\boxed{1}.",
